<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
    Oxide for bat.

    Matches the oxide theme used by vim (vim/colors/oxide.vim), the pi agent
    (pi/agent/themes/oxide.json), Sublime Text (sublime/plugins/Oxide), ghostty
    (ghostty/themes/oxide) and the zsh prompt
    (oh-my-zsh/custom/themes/oxide.zsh-theme).

    This is a port of sublime/plugins/Oxide/Oxide.sublime-color-scheme, which is
    the authoritative version: it carries the reasoning behind every colour
    assignment, and this file carries only what differs. The two are maintained
    in parallel, the same way the vim and pi themes are. Anything changed there
    should be changed here.

    Format
    ======
    bat reads .tmTheme only. It silently ignores .sublime-color-scheme files
    placed in this directory, which is why this is XML rather than a copy of the
    Sublime file. Everything the Sublime scheme relies on does survive the
    translation, because bat highlights through syntect, which implements
    Sublime's selector syntax rather than TextMate's original: descendant
    selectors, the exclusion operator, parenthesised alternation and the scoring
    rules are all honoured. Only the container changed.

    Installing
    ==========
    setup.sh links this whole directory to ~/.config/bat, and carries the exact
    commands as shell comments, where flag names can be written literally (XML
    comments cannot contain a double hyphen, which is why flags are named
    bare below).

    The one thing worth knowing here: bat reads themes from a binary cache
    rather than from disk, so it will not see this file until the cache is
    rebuilt, and will not see edits to it afterwards either. Rebuild with bat's
    "cache" subcommand and its build flag, then confirm with the list-themes
    flag. To make it the default, set theme="Oxide" in ~/.config/bat/config or
    export BAT_THEME=Oxide.

    One trap: the "cache" subcommand silently disappears when the working
    directory contains a file or directory named "cache", because bat resolves
    the argument as a filename first. It reports the build flag as an unexpected
    argument, which does not point at the cause.

    What bat cannot render
    ======================
    Three things in the Sublime scheme have no equivalent here, and the gaps are
    in bat rather than in the translation:

    1.  Per token backgrounds are dropped. bat paints foregrounds only and lets
        the terminal background show through, so a rule setting just a
        background renders as nothing at all. The affected rules are kept below
        anyway, because other syntect consumers (delta) do honour them and
        because dropping them would make this file diverge from the Sublime one
        for no gain. Where a rule sets both, the foreground still applies.

        This is also why the global background matters less than it looks: bat
        never paints it. It is set correctly regardless, and it happens to be
        exactly the background ghostty/themes/oxide paints, so oxide under bat
        in ghostty comes out right by construction.

    2.  Italics need opting into. bat suppresses them unless you pass the
        italic-text=always flag, because not every terminal renders them.
        Comments,
        the receiver (self, this) and blockquotes all want italic; without the
        flag they keep their colour and lose the slant. Worth setting in
        ~/.config/bat/config alongside the theme.

    3.  Some bundled syntaxes are older than Sublime Text's. bat vendors its
        own copies, and a few predate scope names the Sublime scheme relies on.
        Where that changes a colour, a compensating rule appears below with the
        detail; see "Config key (legacy syntaxes)".

    4.  Sublime's own panels have no counterpart. The rules for its inline
        buffer diff (the diff.inserted family), its build output (message.*) and
        its find results (constant.numeric.line-number) are dropped here. They
        colour Sublime UI, not file content, so they would be dead weight. The
        markup.inserted family is a different thing and is kept: that colours
        actual diff files, which is exactly what bat is often pointed at.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>name</key>
    <string>Oxide</string>
    <key>settings</key>
    <array>

        <!-- Globals. Of these bat reads foreground, gutterForeground (line
             numbers under style=numbers) and lineHighlight (the -H flag). The
             rest describe editor furniture bat has no concept of, and are here
             so the file stays a faithful port usable by other syntect hosts. -->
        <dict>
            <key>settings</key>
            <dict>
                <key>background</key><string>#2e3436</string>
                <key>foreground</key><string>#d7d7d7</string>
                <key>accent</key><string>#5fafaf</string>
                <key>caret</key><string>#d7d7d7</string>
                <key>invisibles</key><string>#585d5e</string>

                <key>lineHighlight</key><string>#383f41</string>
                <key>selection</key><string>#626768</string>
                <key>selectionBorder</key><string>#626768</string>
                <key>inactiveSelection</key><string>#4d5659</string>
                <key>misspelling</key><string>#d75f5f</string>
                <key>shadow</key><string>#00000040</string>

                <!-- Line numbers sit on 'border', one step dimmer than
                     comments, so they recede further than anything you are
                     meant to read. Sublime raises the current line's number to
                     body text; bat has no current line, so the dim value is all
                     of it. This is the same value vim's oxide gives LineNr. -->
                <key>gutter</key><string>#2e3436</string>
                <key>gutterForeground</key><string>#585d5e</string>

                <key>guide</key><string>#424a4d</string>
                <key>activeGuide</key><string>#5fafaf</string>
                <key>stackGuide</key><string>#5fafaf80</string>

                <key>highlight</key><string>#5fafaf</string>
                <key>findHighlight</key><string>#afaf87</string>
                <key>findHighlightForeground</key><string>#2e3436</string>

                <key>bracketsForeground</key><string>#d7d7d7</string>
                <key>bracketsOptions</key><string>underline</string>
                <key>bracketContentsForeground</key><string>#5fafaf</string>
                <key>bracketContentsOptions</key><string>underline</string>
                <key>tagsForeground</key><string>#d7af5f</string>
                <key>tagsOptions</key><string>stippled_underline</string>
            </dict>
        </dict>

        <!-- Comments -->
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>Comment</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>comment, punctuation.definition.comment</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict>
                <key>foreground</key><string>#777b7c</string>
                <key>fontStyle</key><string>italic</string>
            </dict>
        </dict>
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>TODO / FIXME</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>comment keyword.codetag, comment meta.toc-list.banner</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict>
                <key>foreground</key><string>#d7af5f</string>
                <key>fontStyle</key><string>bold italic</string>
            </dict>
        </dict>

        <!-- Literals. Purple is for values the language hands you already made:
             numbers, booleans, None, character literals. Strings get their own
             colour because they are the one literal you read as prose. -->
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>Constant</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>constant</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict><key>foreground</key><string>#af87d7</string></dict>
        </dict>
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>String</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>string, punctuation.definition.string</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict><key>foreground</key><string>#afaf87</string></dict>
        </dict>
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>Escape sequence</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>constant.character.escape, constant.other.placeholder</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict><key>foreground</key><string>#d7af5f</string></dict>
        </dict>
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>Regex special</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>string.regexp keyword.operator, string.regexp keyword.control, string.regexp constant.other.character-class</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict><key>foreground</key><string>#d7af5f</string></dict>
        </dict>
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>Interpolation</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>punctuation.section.interpolation, punctuation.definition.template-expression</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict><key>foreground</key><string>#d7af5f</string></dict>
        </dict>
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>Numeric suffix / base</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>storage.type.numeric, punctuation.definition.numeric.base</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict><key>foreground</key><string>#af87d7</string></dict>
        </dict>

        <!-- Names. The accents divide on whether a name is being introduced or
             reached for: limegreen for the definition site, turquoise for a
             name used as a callable or a type and for the names of slots,
             orangeSubtle for parameter names at either end, offWhite for
             everything else. The full argument is in the Sublime scheme. -->
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>Variable</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>variable, variable.member, variable.other</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict><key>foreground</key><string>#d7d7d7</string></dict>
        </dict>
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>Parameter</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>variable.parameter</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict><key>foreground</key><string>#c29356</string></dict>
        </dict>
        <!-- The receiver reads as the parameter it effectively is. The italic
             is what separates it from an ordinary parameter now that they share
             a colour, so under bat this rule needs italic-text=always to carry
             its full meaning; without it self and a plain parameter look
             identical. The descendant selector catches self in a def
             signature, where the parameter rule above would strip the italic. -->
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>Language variable</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>variable.language, variable.parameter variable.language</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict>
                <key>foreground</key><string>#c29356</string>
                <key>fontStyle</key><string>italic</string>
            </dict>
        </dict>
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>Type parameter</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>variable.parameter.type</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict><key>foreground</key><string>#87af5f</string></dict>
        </dict>
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>Function call</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>variable.function, variable.annotation</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict><key>foreground</key><string>#5fafaf</string></dict>
        </dict>
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>Definition site</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>entity.name.function, entity.name.class, entity.name.struct, entity.name.enum, entity.name.union, entity.name.trait, entity.name.interface, entity.name.impl, entity.name.type, entity.name.namespace, entity.name.constant, entity.name.label, entity.name.goto-label</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict><key>foreground</key><string>#87af5f</string></dict>
        </dict>
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>Other entity names</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>entity.name - (entity.name.section | entity.name.tag)</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict><key>foreground</key><string>#87af5f</string></dict>
        </dict>

        <!-- Types and builtins. Turquoise means "a name the language gave you":
             types, exceptions and builtins alike. -->
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>Library type / function / constant</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>support.type, support.class, support.function, support.macro, support.module, support.other, support.constant</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict><key>foreground</key><string>#5fafaf</string></dict>
        </dict>
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>Inherited class</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>entity.other.inherited-class</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict><key>foreground</key><string>#5fafaf</string></dict>
        </dict>

        <!-- Keywords and operators. Import directives are keywords, not a
             category of their own, so they sit on red with everything else.
             That leaves turquoise meaning exactly one thing. -->
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>Keyword</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>keyword, storage, storage.type, storage.modifier</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict><key>foreground</key><string>#d75f5f</string></dict>
        </dict>
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>Operator</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>keyword.operator</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict><key>foreground</key><string>#d75f5f</string></dict>
        </dict>
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>Preprocessor</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>meta.preprocessor, keyword.control.import, keyword.control.export, keyword.other.import</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict><key>foreground</key><string>#d75f5f</string></dict>
        </dict>

        <!-- Punctuation -->
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>Punctuation</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>punctuation, punctuation.separator, punctuation.terminator, punctuation.accessor, punctuation.section</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict><key>foreground</key><string>#777b7c</string></dict>
        </dict>
        <!-- The @ of a decorator marks a language construct the way def or
             class does, so it takes the keyword colour. Only the marker is red;
             the name after it keeps whatever it is. -->
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>Annotation punctuation</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>punctuation.definition.annotation, punctuation.definition.decorator</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict><key>foreground</key><string>#d75f5f</string></dict>
        </dict>

        <!-- Markup and structured data -->
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>Tag name</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>entity.name.tag</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict><key>foreground</key><string>#d75f5f</string></dict>
        </dict>
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>Tag attribute</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>entity.other.attribute-name</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict><key>foreground</key><string>#5fafaf</string></dict>
        </dict>
        <!-- One rule covers Python and JS object literals, JSON and YAML, which
             all scope the key as meta.mapping.key. The quotes rule below must
             always agree with this one: without it the quotes fall to the
             String rule, which names punctuation.definition.string explicitly
             and so out-specifies the plain selector on exactly those two
             characters, giving a quoted key khaki quotes around a turquoise
             name. -->
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>Mapping key</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>meta.mapping.key string - meta.mapping.key meta meta | meta.mapping.key meta.mapping.key string - meta.mapping.key meta.mapping.key meta meta</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict><key>foreground</key><string>#5fafaf</string></dict>
        </dict>
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>Mapping key quotes</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>meta.mapping.key punctuation.definition.string - meta.mapping.key meta meta</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict><key>foreground</key><string>#5fafaf</string></dict>
        </dict>
        <!-- JavaScript and TypeScript object literal keys, which bat's older
             syntax scopes meta.object-literal.key rather than meta.mapping.key.
             The second rule is the same quote fix the mapping key rules need,
             for the same reason: the String rule names
             punctuation.definition.string explicitly and would otherwise claim
             the quotes of a quoted key. -->
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>Object literal key (legacy syntax)</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>meta.object-literal.key string, meta.object-literal.key</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict><key>foreground</key><string>#5fafaf</string></dict>
        </dict>
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>Object literal key quotes (legacy syntax)</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>meta.object-literal.key punctuation.definition.string</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict><key>foreground</key><string>#5fafaf</string></dict>
        </dict>

        <!-- Call sites in the same older syntaxes, which name the callee
             entity.name.function rather than variable.function. The Definition
             site rule claims that scope, so without this every method call in
             JS and TS renders limegreen, as though it were being defined. The
             meta.function-call ancestor is what distinguishes the two: it wraps
             uses, never definitions. Python is unaffected either way; its
             syntax already says variable.function. -->
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>Function call (legacy syntax)</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>meta.function-call entity.name.function</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict><key>foreground</key><string>#5fafaf</string></dict>
        </dict>

        <!-- Keys in YAML, TOML and INI, which reach turquoise by a different
             route. bat bundles older versions of these three syntaxes than
             Sublime Text ships, and they predate meta.mapping.key: they scope a
             key as entity.name.tag (YAML, TOML) or keyword.name (INI) instead.
             Under the rules above that lands a key on red, the keyword colour,
             which is the one clearly wrong answer. This rule is the mirror
             image of a dead entity.name.tag.yaml rule removed from the Sublime
             scheme, where the modern syntax makes it unreachable; here it is
             the only thing that works. It sits below Tag name and is more
             specific, so real HTML and XML tags keep red.

             The decorator marker in JS and TS is the one thing here that cannot
             be recovered. bat's syntax does not model decorators at all: it
             leaves the @ as bare punctuation and the name after it as an
             ordinary variable, so there is no scope to select. Python
             decorators are unaffected and stay red.

             This fixes plain keys only. bat's YAML syntax gives a quoted key no
             key scope at all, so "a": 1 renders its key khaki, as a string. No
             theme can distinguish it; the syntax discards the information
             before the theme is consulted. -->
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>Config key (legacy syntaxes)</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>entity.name.tag.yaml, entity.name.tag.toml, keyword.name</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict><key>foreground</key><string>#5fafaf</string></dict>
        </dict>
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>CSS property name</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>support.type.property-name</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict><key>foreground</key><string>#5fafaf</string></dict>
        </dict>

        <!-- Invalid. The backgrounds here are among the rules bat drops; the
             foregrounds still apply, which is enough to read them. -->
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>Invalid</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>invalid</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict>
                <key>foreground</key><string>#d75f5f</string>
                <key>background</key><string>#4c3a3b</string>
            </dict>
        </dict>
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>Invalid deprecated</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>invalid.deprecated</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict>
                <key>foreground</key><string>#d7af5f</string>
                <key>background</key><string>#4c3a3b</string>
            </dict>
        </dict>

        <!-- Prose -->
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>Heading</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>markup.heading, entity.name.section</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict>
                <key>foreground</key><string>#d7d7d7</string>
                <key>fontStyle</key><string>bold</string>
            </dict>
        </dict>
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>Heading punctuation</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>markup.heading punctuation.definition.heading</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict><key>foreground</key><string>#d75f5f</string></dict>
        </dict>
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>Link</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>markup.underline.link, string.other.link</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict>
                <key>foreground</key><string>#5fafaf</string>
                <key>fontStyle</key><string>underline</string>
            </dict>
        </dict>
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>Link title</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>meta.link string, meta.image string</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict><key>foreground</key><string>#777b7c</string></dict>
        </dict>
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>Bold</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>markup.bold</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict><key>fontStyle</key><string>bold</string></dict>
        </dict>
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>Italic</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>markup.italic</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict><key>fontStyle</key><string>italic</string></dict>
        </dict>
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>Underline</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>markup.underline</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict><key>fontStyle</key><string>underline</string></dict>
        </dict>
        <!-- Neither Sublime nor syntect has a strikethrough style, so struck
             text borrows underline in both. -->
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>Strikethrough</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>markup.strikethrough</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict><key>fontStyle</key><string>underline</string></dict>
        </dict>
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>Bold/italic markers</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>text punctuation.definition.bold, text punctuation.definition.italic</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict><key>foreground</key><string>#af87d7</string></dict>
        </dict>
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>List bullet</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>markup.list punctuation.definition.list_item, markup.list.numbered.bullet</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict><key>foreground</key><string>#af87d7</string></dict>
        </dict>
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>Blockquote</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>markup.quote, markup.quote punctuation.definition.blockquote</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict>
                <key>foreground</key><string>#777b7c</string>
                <key>fontStyle</key><string>italic</string>
            </dict>
        </dict>
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>Thematic break</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>punctuation.definition.thematic-break</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict><key>foreground</key><string>#585d5e</string></dict>
        </dict>
        <!-- Background only, so bat renders a fenced block exactly as it
             renders the surrounding prose. Kept for parity with the Sublime
             scheme and for hosts that do paint it. -->
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>Fenced code block</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>markup.raw</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict><key>background</key><string>#383f41</string></dict>
        </dict>
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>Inline code</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>markup.raw.inline</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict>
                <key>foreground</key><string>#8eac72</string>
                <key>background</key><string>#383f41</string>
            </dict>
        </dict>

        <!-- Diffs. These colour diff files, which bat is often pointed at
             directly (git show | bat, or bat a .patch). Sublime's separate
             rules for its own inline buffer diff are dropped; see the header. -->
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>Diff header</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>meta.diff, meta.diff.header</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict><key>foreground</key><string>#87af5f</string></dict>
        </dict>
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>Diff range</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>meta.diff.range, punctuation.definition.range.diff</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict>
                <key>foreground</key><string>#d7d7d7</string>
                <key>fontStyle</key><string>bold</string>
            </dict>
        </dict>
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>Diff inserted</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>markup.inserted</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict><key>foreground</key><string>#87af5f</string></dict>
        </dict>
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>Diff deleted</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>markup.deleted</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict><key>foreground</key><string>#d75f5f</string></dict>
        </dict>
        <dict>
            <key>name</key><string>Diff changed</string>
            <key>scope</key><string>markup.changed</string>
            <key>settings</key><dict><key>foreground</key><string>#5fafaf</string></dict>
        </dict>

    </array>
</dict>
</plist>
